# Cloudflare Pages edge headers for the public site.
#
# SYNTAX. Pages reads this file from the root of the SERVED directory, which is
# the build output. It therefore lives in public/, so the build copies it to
# dist/_headers. (functions/ is the opposite case: Pages reads that from the
# project root, not from the output.) A rule is a path pattern at column 0
# followed by indented `Name: value` lines; a comment is a line beginning with
# `#` at column 0. Cloudflare documents comments only at column 0 and says
# nothing about indented ones, so every comment here sits at column 0 and the
# rule block below contains header lines and nothing else. Malformed lines are
# ignored rather than reported, so a header written at column 0 by accident
# becomes a path pattern carrying no headers and does nothing, silently.
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM THE PREVIOUS SITE, AND WHY. The old policy was
# `default-src 'none'` with style-src and img-src, and it was true: those pages
# carried zero <script> tags. This site is built from React and ships bundled
# JavaScript, and since 2026-09-05 it carries one background clip, so exactly
# TWO classes of thing are newly permitted — same-origin scripts and same-origin
# media — and everything else is held or tightened. The owner's decision of
# 2026-09-04 is "self-hosted React", and the property that decision must not
# cost is "this site makes no external request". That property is stated below
# in a form the browser enforces: every new directive is `'self'` and nothing is
# `*`, `data:` or `blob:` that was not already.
#
# DIRECTIVE BY DIRECTIVE.
#
# default-src 'none'
#   The deny-by-default floor is unchanged. Every fetch directive not named
#   below inherits it. A directive that is not written here is 'none'.
#
# script-src 'self'
#   THE ONE RELAXATION. The built module scripts, served from this origin. No
#   'unsafe-inline' and no 'unsafe-eval', which has two consequences the build
#   is configured around: Vite's modulepreload polyfill is an inline script, so
#   `build.modulePreload.polyfill` is false in vite.config.ts; and any library
#   that evaluates a string at runtime cannot be used here, whatever it does.
#   There is no inline <script> and no `on*=` attribute anywhere in the output.
#
# style-src 'self'
#   The one built stylesheet, same-origin. Deliberately still no
#   'unsafe-inline': React sets styles through the CSSOM, which is not governed
#   by this directive, so animation works without it. An inline `style=`
#   attribute in the markup would be refused — and there are none.
#
# img-src 'self' data:
#   Same-origin images: the favicons and the social-preview card. `data:` is
#   permitted for images ONLY, because a WebGL hero legitimately builds
#   textures from data URIs. Note the build sets `assetsInlineLimit: 0`, so
#   nothing is inlined by the bundler; this is headroom for the canvas, not a
#   general licence.
#
# media-src IS ABSENT, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE ENTRY.
#   It read `media-src 'self'` until 2026-09-05, for exactly one file:
#   /media/loop-1280.mp4, the ambient clip behind the hero. Revision 2 of the
#   website brief replaced that clip with a drifting point field drawn in the
#   DOM, and the two trailer clips that had been section breaks went with the
#   sections. This origin now serves no video and no audio at all: `public/media`
#   holds two WebP files and nothing else.
#
#   SO THE DIRECTIVE IS DELETED RATHER THAN LEFT PERMITTING NOTHING, and it falls
#   back to `default-src 'none'`. A directive that allows a class of resource the
#   site does not use is a standing permission for the next person to add one
#   without a policy change and therefore without a review. A <video> or <audio>
#   element on this site is now refused by the browser, which is the correct
#   answer for a site that has none.
#
#   TO PUT ONE BACK: add `media-src 'self'` here, in the same commit as the
#   element. NOT `data:` and NOT `blob:`: a media element that can be fed a blob
#   URL is one that can be fed bytes this origin never served, which is exactly
#   the property the rest of this file spends its length establishing.
#
# font-src 'self'
#   The three bundled woff2 faces. NOT `data:`: an inlined face would defeat
#   the check that every font is a file this origin served, which is how the
#   "no external request" claim is verified from the network panel. There is no
#   fonts.googleapis.com and no fonts.gstatic.com, and one request to either is
#   a rejection.
#
# connect-src 'self' https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com
#   The homepage reads protocol state directly from the Robinhood Chain public
#   RPC in the reader's own browser, so a claim on the page is checkable by the
#   reader instead of taken on the server's word. Exactly ONE external origin is
#   permitted, and it is that chain's public JSON-RPC endpoint: nothing else is
#   added. The requests it makes are POSTs carrying eth_call and
#   eth_blockNumber, and the endpoint's own CORS preflight allows exactly one
#   request header, content-type, so no other header and no credential can be
#   sent along with them even if a future change tried to add one. This is
#   still the floor for any future fetch: an analytics call or a beacon to any
#   other host is refused by the browser, not merely discouraged by convention.
#   apps/app/src/_headers permits this same one origin for the same reason, so
#   the two surfaces share one network posture rather than two policies that
#   could drift apart.
#
# worker-src 'self'
#   WebGL itself needs no worker, but a same-origin worker is permitted so the
#   hero can move heavy work off the main thread without a policy change. No
#   `blob:`: a blob worker is the standard way to smuggle in evaluated code.
#
# base-uri 'none'
#   Every link is relative. An injected `<base href="https://evil.example/">`
#   would repoint all of them without a line of JavaScript; this refuses it.
#
# form-action 'none'
#   There is no form on this site and no place to submit one. If HTML injection
#   ever puts one here — a credential prompt on a page carrying the protocol's
#   own disclaimers is the obvious abuse — it has nowhere to POST to.
#
# frame-ancestors 'none'
#   Nobody may frame these pages. The material value is not clickjacking but
#   framing: the footer's legal paragraph is load-bearing legal text — it opens
#   with the not-an-offer disclaimer and the deployment-status sentence, and
#   since 2026-09-04 it is the only place every page states them — and a hostile
#   page that frames this site with the footer cropped out has published the
#   protocol's own words without its disclosures.
#
# object-src 'none'
#   Written explicitly even though default-src already covers it. Plugin
#   content is the one bypass that survives a strict script policy in older
#   engines, and a reader of this file should not have to derive its absence.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
#   This origin serves .html, .css, .js, .woff2, .txt, .xml, .svg, .ico, .png
#   and .webp. `.mp4` left the list on 2026-09-05 with the last video file. Sniffing is the mechanism by which a file served as one type
#   is interpreted as another; nosniff makes the declared Content-Type binding.
#   The last two joined the list on 2026-09-05 with the section backdrops.
#
# Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
#   Off-site links go only to the project repository. Without this, following
#   one tells github.com which page a visitor was reading, and /risks or
#   /operators is a meaningful signal about a reader that this site has no
#   reason to disclose. There is no analytics, so the strictest value costs
#   nothing.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

# The build emits content-hashed filenames under /assets/, so those files are
# immutable by construction: a change produces a new name rather than new bytes
# at the same name. The HTML is not hashed and must not be cached that way.
#
# THAT SENTENCE IS A RULE ABOUT WHAT MAY LIVE UNDER /assets/, not just a note.
# Vite hashes what it processes; it copies public/ through verbatim, path and
# all. So a file placed at public/assets/ serves from /assets/ with a FIXED name
# and inherits a year of immutable caching it cannot satisfy — replacing it
# would leave every cache that already holds it serving the old bytes until
# 2027, with no way to bust it short of renaming. The social-preview card was
# at public/assets/og-card.png for exactly that reason and was moved to
# public/og-card.png on 2026-09-04; the og:image and twitter:image URLs on all
# eight pages point at https://rwally.com/og-card.png. Nothing unhashed belongs
# under this prefix. Put a verbatim public asset at the root of public/.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# /media/ HAS NO CACHE RULE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The backdrops and the
# clip are copied verbatim out of public/, so their names are fixed rather than
# content-hashed — which is precisely the property the paragraph above says
# disqualifies a file from the /assets/ prefix. A fixed name cannot carry a year
# of immutable caching it has no way to bust. So these files take the origin's
# default freshness, which for Cloudflare Pages is a short cache the edge
# revalidates. The visual set is still being redrawn; a slot that can be
# replaced is worth more here than a byte saved on the second visit.
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Each of these is a standard header that would do nothing
# here, and a header that does nothing is a claim of protection the next reader
# has to disprove:
#
#   X-Frame-Options    -- superseded by frame-ancestors in every browser that
#                         ships CSP Level 2.
#   Permissions-Policy -- gates browser APIs. Worth revisiting if the hero ever
#                         wants a device sensor; it wants none today, and a
#                         policy listing every feature this site does not use
#                         is a list nobody maintains.
#   X-XSS-Protection   -- removed from every current browser; its legacy
#                         scanner introduced vulnerabilities of its own.
#
# STRICT-TRANSPORT-SECURITY REMAINS THE OWNER'S DECISION, NOT THIS FILE'S, and
# is left commented out for the same reason as on the previous site: HSTS is
# close to irreversible, and the zone may already set it under Cloudflare
# SSL/TLS -> Edge Certificates, where two sources would disagree. The ramp, if
# the owner wants it: max-age=86400, then 31536000 on the apex, then
# includeSubDomains once every intended subdomain is HTTPS-only, then preload
# as a deliberate decision to accept the removal process.
#
#   INDENT IT UNDER A PATH PATTERN WHEN ENABLING. The line below sits at column
#   0, and an uncommented line at column 0 is a PATH PATTERN, not a header.
#
#       Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=86400
